In the batch processing loop over journal files, calling `statSync` directly without error handling can throw an exception if a file is deleted or unreadable. This would halt the entire journal loading operation and fail-fast, violating the resilient continuation design intent. Wrapping `statSync` in a `try/catch` block ensures that a single problematic file does not prevent other valid journals from being processed.

```javascript
        for (const f of files) {
          let mtimeMs = -Infinity;
          try {
            mtimeMs = statSync(join(journalDir, f)).mtimeMs;
          } catch (err) {
            process.stderr.write(
              '[session-context] Warning: Could not stat journal file ' + f + ': ' + err.message + '\n'
            );
          }
          if (mtimeMs > newestMtimeMs) {
            newestMtimeMs = mtimeMs;
            mtimeLatest = f;
          }
        }
```

